    "title": "What we do",
    "minutes": 15,
    "summary": "The job in eight lines, the unfavorable record rule, and where opinions belong.",
    "html": "<ul><li>Read and summarize public documents, and hand people the citation</li><li>Answer specific false claims with specific sources</li><li>Get the truth out fast when a commissioner, staff member, or project is attacked with something inaccurate</li><li>Say &ldquo;we don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; when the record is silent, and then go find out</li><li>Publish a correction when we get something wrong</li><li>Explain the record to anyone who asks, whatever their views</li><li>Attend public meetings, take notes, and speak in public comment</li><li>Keep our own sourcing good enough that nobody can dismiss us</li></ul>\n<h2>The Unfavorable Record Rule</h2>\n<p>If the documented record contradicts something the group has published, <b>we say so.</b> We publish the correction with the same effort we published the original.</p>\n<p>This is not a nice-to-have. A group that only corrects other people&rsquo;s errors is a partisan operation with a research department. A group that corrects its own is a source. The second one is what we are building, and it is the only version of this that survives contact with a skeptical neighbor.</p>\n<h2>Facts and Opinions</h2>\n<p>Two different things, and both are ours to say.</p>\n<p><b>A fact we publish</b> carries a citation. That is what makes it worth more than the rumor it is answering. If we cannot produce the document, it does not go on the fact sheet.</p>\n<p><b>An opinion</b> is what any of us thinks about what the record shows, and every volunteer is free to say it — on a porch, at the podium, at the mailbox, in a letter over their own name. We are neighbors and citizens with the same right to speak as anyone else in this town, and nobody signs that away by joining this group.</p>\n<p>The only thing we keep straight is which is which. &ldquo;The adopted budget shows the line at $1.2 million, page 14&rdquo; and &ldquo;I think the people screaming about it know better and don&rsquo;t care&rdquo; are both things a volunteer can say. One comes with a page number. Don&rsquo;t put the second one on the fact sheet, because the fact sheet is the thing that has to be unarguable.</p>"
