    "title": "The Record Verification Standard",
    "minutes": 35,
    "summary": "Tier 1 and 2 sources, what is not a source, the two-person rule, and citation format.",
    "html": "<p>Everything the group publishes as fact runs through this standard. It is the core of the operation.</p>\n<h2>Tier 1: Acceptable Primary Sources</h2>\n<ul><li>Meeting minutes, agendas, and official video/audio of public bodies</li><li>Ordinances, resolutions, and adopted budgets</li><li>Filed court documents and case dockets</li><li>Public records obtained by request (Florida Chapter 119 request)</li><li>Financial disclosures and filings on file with a government office</li><li>Contracts, permits, and inspection records held by a public agency</li><li>Direct, complete quotations from a public meeting, with timestamp</li></ul>\n<h2>Tier 2: Usable With Attribution</h2>\n<ul><li>News reporting from an identified outlet, cited as reporting rather than as fact</li><li>Official statements from an agency&rsquo;s own website or release</li><li>Documents provided by a named person who will stand behind them</li></ul>\n<h2>Not Sources</h2>\n<ul><li>Social media posts, screenshots, and forwarded messages</li><li>&ldquo;Someone at the meeting told me&rdquo;</li><li>Another group&rsquo;s summary of a document</li><li>Our own memory of a meeting we attended</li><li>Anything a volunteer cannot produce on request</li></ul>\n<h2>The Two-Person Rule</h2>\n<p>No claim is published as fact until two volunteers have each independently:</p>\n<ol><li>Retrieved the source document themselves</li><li>Confirmed the claim is supported by the document as written</li><li>Confirmed the citation is specific enough for a stranger to find it</li><li>Signed the Verification Log entry</li></ol>\n<h2>Citation Format</h2>\n<p>Every published fact carries a citation a neighbor can act on:</p>\n<div class=\"say\"><div class=\"l\">Say this</div><p>City of Apopka, Regular City Council Meeting Minutes, March 4, 2026, Item 7B, page 4. Available at apopka.gov under Agendas &amp; Minutes, or by request at City Clerk&rsquo;s office.</p></div>\n<p>Not: &ldquo;City records show...&rdquo; Not: &ldquo;According to public documents...&rdquo; Those are unverifiable, and unverifiable is what the other side sounds like.</p>\n<h2>Handling Ambiguity</h2>\n<p>When the record is genuinely unclear, we say exactly that, in writing:</p>\n<div class=\"say\"><div class=\"l\">Say this</div><p>The minutes record the vote but do not record the discussion. The audio recording for this session is not posted. We have requested it. We do not know what was said.</p></div>\n<p>&ldquo;We don&rsquo;t know yet&rdquo; published honestly builds more credibility than a confident guess ever will.</p>\n<h2>Our Own Corrections</h2>\n<p>If we publish something wrong:</p>\n<ol><li>The Record Director issues a written correction within 72 hours</li><li>It goes out through every channel the original went out through</li><li>It is stated plainly: &ldquo;We got this wrong. Here is what the record actually says.&rdquo;</li><li>The Verification Log records what failed and why</li></ol>"
