    "minutes": 20,
    "summary": "The working center of the group. Who you call, how fast, and every door the answer goes through.",
    "html": "<p>This is the working center of the group. A false claim about the City gets answered, and it gets answered fast enough to matter.</p>\n<h2>The Response Path</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Any volunteer reports it the same day</b>to their Team Lead, in the exact words they heard it — at the mailbox, in a meeting, on Facebook, wherever it surfaced.</li><li><b>Team Lead forwards to the Record Director</b>immediately.</li><li><b>Record Director identifies the one document</b>that settles it and pulls it — website, City Clerk, or Chapter 119 request.</li><li><b>Two-person verification.</b></li><li><b>The finding goes to every volunteer within 72 hours</b>, with the citation and a plain sentence they can say out loud.</li><li><b>It goes out through every channel at once</b>conversations, the fact sheet, the Facebook page, public comment at the next meeting, a letter to the editor from whoever wants to write one.</li></ol>\n<p><b>72 hours with a document beats 6 hours with a guess.</b> If the document is not in hand yet, what goes out is: &ldquo;We&rsquo;ve heard this. We&rsquo;ve requested the records. We&rsquo;ll publish what they say.&rdquo; That holds the ground honestly and costs us nothing.</p>\n<h2>Getting the Truth Out</h2>\n<p>The same finding, through every door:</p>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Who</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Neighbor conversations</td><td>Every volunteer</td><td>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got the document on that — want to see it?&rdquo;</td></tr><tr><td>Fact sheet</td><td>Record Director</td><td>New entry, dated version, reprinted</td></tr><tr><td>Facebook page</td><td>Record Director</td><td>One post, one document, citation in the post</td></tr><tr><td>Public comment</td><td>Any volunteer who wants the mic</td><td>Read the document into the record, with the citation</td></tr><tr><td>Letters to the editor</td><td>Any volunteer, own name</td><td>Cite the document; sign it yourself</td></tr><tr><td>Invited presentations</td><td>Team Lead and Educator</td><td>Add it to the three claims we walk through</td></tr><tr><td>Source binder</td><td>Every team</td><td>The printed page, tabbed, ready to hand over</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Speaking at Public Meetings</h2>\n<p>Public comment is the most direct way to put the truth on the record, and it is where the false claims get made in the first place.</p>\n<ul><li>Any volunteer may speak. Nobody needs permission from this group.</li><li>Bring the document and the citation. Read the relevant line.</li><li>Stay inside the time limit so you are not cut off mid-point.</li><li>It goes into the official record, which means it becomes a source we can cite later.</li></ul>\n<h2>When We Are the Ones Attacked</h2>\n<p>If someone comes after this group, the answer is the same as everything else: the documents. Hand over the citation and let them check it. We do not have to win the exchange — we have to be the group whose facts hold up when someone goes and looks, because that is what makes the next hundred conversations easy.</p>\n<p>Report anything that gets personal or threatening to the Overall Coordinator the same day.</p>"
