    "summary": "Ten teams of three, how they form, and the meeting rhythm.",
    "html": "<h2>Ten Teams of Three</h2>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Responsibilities</th><th>Traits to Look For</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Team Lead</b></td><td>Scheduling, coordination, reporting, team support</td><td>Organized, reliable, steady</td></tr><tr><td><b>Educator</b></td><td>Conversations, presentations, answering questions</td><td>Patient, good listener, comfortable saying &ldquo;let me look that up&rdquo;</td></tr><tr><td><b>Recorder</b></td><td>Logging claims heard, questions asked, follow-ups owed</td><td>Detail-oriented, consistent</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Roles are flexible in practice — the Recorder can talk, the Educator can log — but each function must be covered.</p>\n<h2>Team Formation</h2>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"t\">Step 1 — Identify Team Leads (Week 1, Days 1–3)</div></div>\n<p><i>Who to approach:</i> retired professionals with coordination experience, active members of faith or civic groups, people known for follow-through, people who already host gatherings.</p>\n<p><i>How to approach:</i></p>\n<ol><li>One-on-one, in person or by phone, 30 minutes</li><li>Share the mission statement and this plan</li><li>Describe the role: coordinate two other people, set your team&rsquo;s schedule, be the contact point, report weekly</li><li>Ask directly: &ldquo;Would you be willing to lead a team of three?&rdquo;</li><li>If yes — provide the Team Lead Handbook and schedule training</li><li>If no — ask for a referral</li></ol>\n<p><i>Answering the common objections:</i></p>\n<ul><li><i>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t have time.&rdquo;</i> → &ldquo;One to two hours a week. You set the schedule.&rdquo;</li><li><i>&ldquo;I&rsquo;m not political.&rdquo;</i> → &ldquo;We read documents and tell people what they say. If you can look up a meeting agenda, you can do this.&rdquo;</li><li><i>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know enough.&rdquo;</i> → &ldquo;Nobody starts knowing. The job is looking things up, not knowing them already.&rdquo;</li><li><i>&ldquo;What if someone asks me something I can&rsquo;t answer?&rdquo;</i> → &ldquo;You say &rsquo;I don&rsquo;t know, let me find out.&rsquo; That is the correct answer and we train people to use it.&rdquo;</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"t\">Step 2 — Assemble Teams (Week 1, Days 4–7)</div></div>\n<p>Each Team Lead recruits two members from their own network, looking for complementary strengths. Report names, roles, and contact preferences to the Overall Coordinator.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"t\">Step 3 — Establish Team Communication (Week 2, Day 1)</div></div>\n<p>Group text, weekly call, or email thread — Team Lead&rsquo;s choice. Test with all three members. Set a 24-hour response expectation.</p>\n<h2>Weekly Team Check-In (15 minutes)</h2>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Item</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0:00–0:02</td><td>What went well</td></tr><tr><td>0:02–0:07</td><td>Conversations: how many, what claims came up</td></tr><tr><td>0:07–0:10</td><td>Questions we couldn&rsquo;t answer</td></tr><tr><td>0:10–0:15</td><td>Next week: who does what</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Monthly Team Health Check</h2>\n<ul><li>All three members active</li><li>Communication flowing</li><li>Claims and questions being logged</li><li>Conflicts addressed</li><li>Resource needs</li><li>Training needs</li></ul>\n<p>Due to the Overall Coordinator by the 1st of each month.</p>"
