    "title": "Role-play scenarios",
    "minutes": 20,
    "summary": "Eight conversations, including the opinion question and the disruptor.",
    "html": "<div class=\"scen\"><h4>1 — The Skeptic</h4><p class=\"said\">&ldquo;Every group like this has an angle.&rdquo;</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;Sure we do — we think the City&rsquo;s been getting hammered with stuff that isn&rsquo;t true. That&rsquo;s exactly why every line on this sheet tells you where to look it up yourself. Don&rsquo;t take my word for it, check it.&rdquo;</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>2 — The Confident Misinformer</h4><p class=\"said\">&ldquo;I know for a fact that [claim].&rdquo;</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;Where did you come across it?&rdquo; Listen. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got the document on that one — want to see it?&rdquo; Show the page. Let the document be the thing that disagrees; it is more persuasive than you are and it cannot be argued with.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>3 — The Neighbor Who Is Right</h4><p class=\"said\">Makes a claim the record supports.</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;You&rsquo;re right, and here&rsquo;s the document that backs you up.&rdquo; Then hand them the citation. This scenario is mandatory in every training — it is what makes the rest of the sheet believable.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>4 — The Question We Can&rsquo;t Answer</h4><p class=\"said\">&ldquo;What about [something not in our materials]?&rdquo;</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know. I&rsquo;ll find the record and get back to you.&rdquo; Get contact information if they want the answer. Log it. Actually follow up.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>5 — The Neighbor Who Won&rsquo;t Hear It</h4><p class=\"said\">&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t want to hear it.&rdquo;</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;Fair enough — the sheet&rsquo;s here if you ever want it.&rdquo; Leave it with them if they&rsquo;ll take it. You don&rsquo;t need the last word; you need the citation in their hand.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>6 — The Record Cuts Against Us</h4><p class=\"said\">A neighbor produces a document contradicting something the group published.</p><p class=\"prac\">&ldquo;Let me see that. If we&rsquo;ve got it wrong, we&rsquo;ll publish a correction — can I take a picture of it?&rdquo; Report to the Record Director the same day.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>7 — The Opinion Question</h4><p class=\"said\">&ldquo;So what do you think about the commissioner?&rdquo;</p><p class=\"prac\">Answer however you want — you&rsquo;re a citizen and it&rsquo;s your opinion. Then get to the part that actually moves them: &ldquo;But here&rsquo;s what the minutes say, and you can look it up yourself.&rdquo; The opinion is yours. The document is the thing that changes their mind.</p></div>\n<div class=\"scen\"><h4>8 — The Disruptor in the Room</h4><p class=\"said\">One of the regular attackers confronts you at a meeting or an event.</p><p class=\"prac\">You&rsquo;re not obligated to engage, and you&rsquo;re not obligated to stay quiet. If you answer, answer with the document: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s in the March 4 minutes, page 4 — go read it.&rdquo; Then go back to what you were doing. The people you&rsquo;re actually talking to are the fifty neighbors watching, not the person in front of you. Report anything threatening to the Coordinator the same day.</p></div>"
