    "summary": "The 2.5-hour training, module by module.",
    "html": "<h2>Training</h2>\n<p><b>Location options:</b> Apopka Community Center, church fellowship hall, library meeting room, private home.</p>\n<p><b>Materials per volunteer:</b> mission card, Record Verification Standard card, Fact Sheet, Claim Response card, Claim Log forms, safety card, name tag.</p>\n<h2>Training Agenda (2.5 hours)</h2>\n<div class=\"tscroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Module</th><th>Lead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0:00–0:10</td><td>Welcome and introductions</td><td>Overall Coordinator</td></tr><tr><td>0:10–0:25</td><td>Mission and focus</td><td>Overall Coordinator</td></tr><tr><td>0:25–1:00</td><td>The Record Verification Standard</td><td>Record Director</td></tr><tr><td>1:00–1:30</td><td>How to have the conversation</td><td>Training Lead</td></tr><tr><td>1:30–1:45</td><td>Answering a false claim fast</td><td>Record Director</td></tr><tr><td>1:45–2:00</td><td>Logging claims and questions</td><td>Data Manager</td></tr><tr><td>2:00–2:20</td><td>Role-play</td><td>All</td></tr><tr><td>2:20–2:30</td><td>Safety and next steps</td><td>Training Lead</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Module 1 — Mission and Focus (15 min)</h2>\n<p>Read the mission aloud. Walk the What We Do list. Cover the Unfavorable Record Rule explicitly so nobody is surprised by it later, and cover the Facts and Opinions distinction — what goes on the fact sheet needs a citation; what a volunteer says as their own view is their own business.</p>\n<p><i>Activity:</i> pairs explain the mission to each other in their own words.</p>\n<h2>Module 2 — The Record Verification Standard (35 min)</h2>\n<p>Walk through Tier 1, Tier 2, and Not Sources. Demonstrate live: pull up a real Apopka agenda on a phone or laptop and show the group how to find it, cite it, and hand the citation to someone else.</p>\n<p><i>Activity:</i> each volunteer looks up one public document and writes a proper citation for it. Review as a group. Anyone who cannot produce a retrievable citation gets help before leaving.</p>\n<h2>Module 3 — How to Have the Conversation (30 min)</h2>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"t\">The four-step conversation:</div></div>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><b>Ask</b>&ldquo;What have you heard about it?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Listen</b>All the way to the end.</li><li><b>Locate</b>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ve got the document on that. Want to see it?&rdquo;</li><li><b>Hand off</b>Give them the citation so they can check it themselves.</li></ol>\n<p><b>You don&rsquo;t have to win it.</b> If someone rejects the document, you can leave it there: &ldquo;It&rsquo;s a public record, so it&rsquo;ll be there if you ever want to look.&rdquo; A neighbor who walks away holding a citation will look it up that night, and that is the whole objective.</p>\n<p><b>&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; is a complete answer.</b> Followed by: &ldquo;I&rsquo;ll find out and get back to you.&rdquo; Then actually do it. This single habit does more for the group&rsquo;s credibility than any fact sheet.</p>\n<p><b>When the record is unfavorable to what we&rsquo;d prefer:</b> say it plainly. &ldquo;You&rsquo;re right about that part — here&rsquo;s the document.&rdquo; Volunteers who see this modeled in training will do it in the field, and it is why the rest of the sheet gets believed.</p>\n<p><i>Activity:</i> role-play using Appendix C scenarios, including at minimum one where the neighbor is correct.</p>\n<h2>Module 4 — Answering a False Claim Fast (15 min)</h2>\n<p>Walk the response path in Defending Against False Claims start to finish with a real example. Every volunteer should leave knowing exactly who they call the same day they hear something, and what happens next.</p>\n<h2>Module 5 — Logging (15 min)</h2>\n<p>What gets recorded and what does not:</p>\n<p><b>Record:</b> date, general setting, how the conversation happened, the specific claim heard, questions asked, whether follow-up is owed.</p>\n<p><b>Do not record:</b> names, addresses, or phone numbers of people we talked to, unless they asked for a follow-up and gave us their contact information.</p>\n<p>We track <b>claims and questions</b>. The log&rsquo;s only purpose is to tell us which false claims are circulating and which documents we need next.</p>"
